# Append-only history files: merge by UNION, never by picking a side.
# =====================================================================
# WHY (measured experimentally 2026-08-19):
#
# Every workflow in this repo reconciles with `git pull --rebase --autostash -X ours`. During a
# REBASE the meaning of "ours" INVERTS: our commits are replayed onto upstream, so `ours` is
# UPSTREAM and `theirs` is the work being replayed. `-X ours` therefore resolves every conflict
# in favour of whoever pushed first and DISCARDS the pushing run's own rows.
#
# Reproduced in a scratch repo. Upstream appended 1 row, our run appended 3:
#
#     -X ours    -> our 3 rows LOST, upstream's kept   (git: "dropping <sha> -- patch
#                                                        contents already upstream")
#     -X theirs  -> our 3 rows kept, upstream's LOST
#     merge=union-> BOTH kept                           <-- correct for append-only
#
# The workflow reports SUCCESS in every case, which is why this was invisible. It matters much
# more now that the NEAR odds captures loop: a lost push used to cost one snapshot, it now costs
# ~100 minutes of closing-line curve, which is the single measurement CLV depends on.
#
# SCOPE — deliberately narrow. `union` concatenates both sides' lines, so it is correct ONLY for
# line-oriented, append-only text. It would produce a syntactically INVALID file if applied to
# JSON (two merged objects) or a binary parquet, and it would DUPLICATE rows in a ledger whose
# existing rows get edited in place — which is why output/bets_ledger.csv is NOT listed here
# despite having three concurrent writers: update_results.py rewrites its rows to fill
# WIN/LOSS/PnL, so union would keep the pre- and post-settlement copy of the same bet.
#
# Safe for the files below because each is written by a script that re-reads the whole file,
# re-sorts chronologically and collapses consecutive-duplicate prices. Any interleaving or
# duplicate line that union creates is therefore normalised on the very next pass.

output/standard_sidemarket_odds_history.csv  merge=union
output/newformat_odds_history.csv            merge=union
output/newformat_odds_dense.csv              merge=union
output/book_odds_snapshots.csv               merge=union
output/api_usage_log.csv                     merge=union

# Keep line endings stable so a Windows local checkout and a Linux runner cannot produce a
# whole-file diff (which would turn every append into a maximal conflict).
*.csv text eol=lf
